Turning repeated command sequences into a vocabulary of your own — the mechanism that every pattern flagged as "covered fully in Part 8" throughout this manual has been building toward.

## Where this fits, and why it's arrived so late in the manual

Custom commands have appeared constantly as forward-references — `cy.loginAs()` in Part 7, `cy.getByCy()` in Part 4, `cy.interceptLeaveApi()` in Part 5, `cy.fillForm()` in Part 3. That ordering is deliberate: a custom command is only a good idea once you actually understand the underlying commands it wraps well enough to know which parts are genuinely reusable and which parts vary per call site. Writing custom commands too early, before you've felt the repetition firsthand, tends to produce premature abstractions that fight you later. This chapter is the payoff chapter — the mechanics behind everything you've already seen used.

## The basic API

```js
Cypress.Commands.add(name, callbackFn);
Cypress.Commands.add(name, options, callbackFn);

// cypress/support/commands.js
Cypress.Commands.add('getByCy', (selector) => {
  return cy.get(`[data-cy=${selector}]`);
});

cy.getByCy('submit').click();
```

Registration happens once, at spec-load time, via support/commands.js — imported by support/e2e.js (Part 1.3) — which is why every custom command is available in every spec without an explicit import in the spec file itself.

## The three command types: parent, child, and dual

Recall from Part 2.3 that built-in commands fall into these categories, and custom commands must declare which they are:

```js
// PARENT — starts a fresh chain, ignores any existing subject
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.session(role, () => { /* ... */ });
});

// CHILD — requires an existing subject, declared via prevSubject
Cypress.Commands.add('shouldBeApproved', { prevSubject: true }, (subject) => {
  cy.wrap(subject).find('[data-cy=status]').should('have.text', 'Approved');
  return subject;    // pass the original subject through, so the chain can continue
});

// DUAL — works both as a starting point AND chained off a subject
Cypress.Commands.add('clickIfExists', { prevSubject: 'optional' }, (subject, selector) => {
  const target = subject ? cy.wrap(subject).find(selector) : cy.get(selector);
  target.then(($el) => { if ($el.length) cy.wrap($el).click(); });
});

cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').first().shouldBeApproved();   // child usage

cy.clickIfExists('[data-cy=cookie-accept]');                 // dual, used as parent
cy.get('[data-cy=modal]').clickIfExists('[data-cy=close]');  // dual, used as child

{ prevSubject: true } means the command requires a prior subject and errors without one. { prevSubject: 'optional' } means it accepts one if present but works standalone too — the subject arrives as undefined in that case, which is exactly what the clickIfExists example branches on. { prevSubject: ['element'] } (an array) further restricts what type of subject is acceptable, throwing a clear error if, say, a string was piped in where a jQuery element was expected.
```
Getting this categorization wrong produces a specific, informative error — Cypress will tell you a command that expected a subject received undefined, or that a parent-style command was chained unexpectedly. This is a case where reading the error message fully genuinely tells you exactly what's misconfigured, rather than requiring guesswork.

## Returning values, and the passthrough convention

For a child command that should continue the chain, the convention — followed by essentially every built-in Cypress command — is to return the subject unchanged unless the command's entire purpose is to transform it:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('logAndContinue', { prevSubject: true }, (subject, message) => {
  cy.log(message);
  return subject;     // the chain continues exactly as before
});

cy.get('[data-cy=row]').logAndContinue('checking this row').find('[data-cy=status]');
```

If you omit the return subject line here, .find() on the next line would fail — the chain would receive undefined as its incoming subject instead of the row element. This is a genuinely common first-time custom-command bug, and it's the exact same passthrough principle from Part 2.3's .then() behavior — a command that returns nothing lets the original subject flow through, but a command that returns undefined explicitly returned from inside an async-style operation does not get that same automatic passthrough; you must return it yourself.

## Commands that end the chain deliberately

Some custom commands are meant to be terminal, like `cy.visit()` or `cy.clearCookies()`:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('resetAndSeedData', () => {
  cy.task('resetDatabase');
  cy.task('seedDefaultEmployees');
  cy.log('Database reset and reseeded');
  // no return value — chaining further off this command isn't meaningful
});
```

## Enqueuing multiple internal steps

A custom command's body can contain as many cy.* commands as needed — they get enqueued into the same command queue (Part 2.3) as if they'd been written directly in the test:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('submitLeaveRequest', (leaveData) => {
  cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').select(leaveData.type);
  cy.get('[data-cy=start-date]').clear().type(leaveData.start);
  cy.get('[data-cy=end-date]').clear().type(leaveData.end);
  if (leaveData.reason) {
    cy.get('[data-cy=reason]').type(leaveData.reason);
  }
  cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
});

cy.submitLeaveRequest({ type: 'Sick Leave', start: '2026-10-01', end: '2026-10-02', reason: 'Flu' });
```

From the calling test's point of view, this behaves exactly like a single command in the Command Log's summary view, but expands to show every internal step — genuinely useful for keeping a test's top-level narrative readable while still exposing full detail when debugging a failure.

## Overloading arguments sensibly

Custom commands that accept an object of options (rather than a long positional argument list) age better as requirements grow:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('submitLeaveRequest', ({ type, start, end, reason, halfDay = false } = {}) => {
  cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').select(type);
  cy.get('[data-cy=start-date]').clear().type(start);
  cy.get('[data-cy=end-date]').clear().type(end);
  if (reason) cy.get('[data-cy=reason]').type(reason);
  if (halfDay) cy.get('[data-cy=half-day]').check();
  cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
});
```

Adding a new optional field (halfDay) here doesn't break any existing call site, whereas a positional-argument version (submitLeaveRequest(type, start, end, reason, halfDay)) would force every caller to be touched or risk silently passing arguments in the wrong slot.

## Cypress.Commands.overwrite() — modifying a built-in command's behavior

Distinct from .add(), .overwrite() replaces the implementation of an existing command — built-in or custom — globally, for the whole project:

```js
Cypress.Commands.overwrite('type', (originalFn, subject, text, options) => {
  // Trim trailing whitespace from every .type() call, project-wide
  return originalFn(subject, text.trimEnd(), options);
});
```

A genuinely common real use — logging every visit for debugging without touching every spec file:

```js
Cypress.Commands.overwrite('visit', (originalFn, url, options) => {
  cy.task('log', `Visiting: ${url}`);
  return originalFn(url, options);
});
```

This is powerful and correspondingly risky. Overwriting a command as fundamental as .click() or .type() changes behavior for the entire suite, including every third-party plugin's internal use of that command — a subtle bug introduced here can manifest as confusing failures in completely unrelated tests. Reach for .overwrite() sparingly, document why clearly at the point of definition, and prefer .add() with a new name whenever the new behavior isn't genuinely meant to apply universally.

## Assertion-style custom commands, chaining correctly with .should()

A command meant to be used the way a built-in assertion is used needs to participate correctly in retry semantics (Part 2.4). The `cy.validateSchema()` example from Part 6.2 demonstrated this pattern; the general shape:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('shouldHaveLeaveBalance', { prevSubject: true }, (subject, expectedBalance) => {
  cy.wrap(subject)
    .find('[data-cy=leave-balance]')
    .should('have.text', String(expectedBalance));
  return subject;
});

cy.get('[data-cy=employee-card]').shouldHaveLeaveBalance(15);
```

Because the internal work is delegated to a real .should() call, this command inherits genuine retry behavior — it isn't a one-shot check masquerading as an assertion.

## Where to draw the line — not every repeated block deserves a command

A pattern worth stating explicitly, because over-abstraction is a real cost: a two-line sequence used in exactly one spec file doesn't need a custom command — the indirection costs more (an extra file to check, a layer between the test and what it's actually doing) than it saves. The threshold that tends to work well in practice: three or more genuinely identical use sites, or one use site whose intent is meaningfully clearer named than inlined (`cy.loginAs('manager')` reads better than four inlined lines of request/storage-setting, even used only twice). Chasing DRY for its own sake in a test suite — unlike in application code — has a real downside: it makes a failing test's Command Log one level more indirect to read, right when clarity matters most.

## TypeScript typing, revisited from Part 1.8

Every custom command should be typed if the project uses TypeScript at all — this was covered mechanically in Part 1.8, and it's worth restating here as a completion discipline: a custom command without a corresponding .d.ts entry is a command with no autocomplete and no compile-time typo protection, which defeats a meaningful part of the reason to adopt TypeScript for a Cypress suite in the first place.

```js
declare global {
  namespace Cypress {
    interface Chainable {
      submitLeaveRequest(data: {
        type: string; start: string; end: string; reason?: string; halfDay?: boolean;
      }): Chainable<void>;
    }
  }
}
```

## Comparison

Playwright's nearest equivalent isn't a single mechanism but a deliberate architectural split: simple reusable action sequences are just plain async functions (await loginAs(page, 'manager')), since test code already runs in ordinary JavaScript with no command-queue constraint to work around — there's no `Cypress.Commands.add` because there's no special registration step needed at all. For genuinely structured, dependency-injected reusable setup, Playwright instead reaches for its fixture system (test.extend({ loggedInPage: async ({ page }, use) => {...} })), which is a categorically different, more powerful mechanism than Cypress custom commands — it handles setup and teardown, and dependencies between fixtures compose automatically. Cypress custom commands have no built-in teardown concept at all; any cleanup a command's caller needs has to be handled explicitly, typically via afterEach (Part 2.1) rather than anything intrinsic to the command itself. This is a genuine, structural difference worth naming plainly: Cypress's reusability story is "a global function registry," Playwright's is "a dependency-injection container with lifecycle management" — the latter is more powerful for complex setups, the former is simpler to reach for on a small suite.
Selenium has no built-in reusability mechanism of any kind — the equivalent of a custom command is simply a function, method, or Page Object method in whatever host language you're using, with exactly the same "just write a function" simplicity as the Playwright comparison, but without either Cypress's command-queue integration or Playwright's fixture lifecycle.

A closer look at `Cypress.Commands.overwrite()` — the sharpest tool in this Part, worth its own chapter because misusing it has the widest blast radius of anything covered here.

## Why this is different in kind from adding a new command

Adding a command (`cy.getByCy`) introduces something new; nothing that previously worked can break, because nothing previously called it. Overwriting a command changes behavior retroactively for every existing call site in the entire codebase, including calls made by third-party plugins your project depends on that you may not have written or even read. This asymmetry is why this chapter treats .overwrite() with more caution than .add().

## The signature

```js
Cypress.Commands.overwrite(name, (originalFn, ...args) => {
  // ...
  return originalFn(...args);
});
```

The first argument your callback receives is always the original implementation — you're not replacing it blind, you're wrapping it, and you almost always want to call it at some point rather than reimplementing the command's entire behavior from scratch.

## Legitimate use 1 — project-wide logging or debugging instrumentation

```js
Cypress.Commands.overwrite('visit', (originalFn, url, options) => {
  cy.task('log', `[${Cypress.spec.name}] Visiting: ${url}`);
  return originalFn(url, options);
});
```

This adds a debugging trace to every navigation across the entire suite without touching a single spec file — genuinely valuable for diagnosing CI-only failures (Part 12.1) where you can't attach a debugger.

## Legitimate use 2 — enforcing a project-wide convention

```js
Cypress.Commands.overwrite('type', (originalFn, subject, text, options) => {
  if (typeof text !== 'string') {
    throw new Error(`cy.type() received a non-string value: ${JSON.stringify(text)}. Did you forget to .invoke('text') first?`);
  }
  return originalFn(subject, text, options);
});
```

This turns a class of mistake (accidentally typing a jQuery object or a number without converting it) into an immediate, clear error at the point of the mistake, rather than a confusing downstream failure. Genuinely useful on a large team where this specific error recurs often enough to be worth guarding against centrally.

## Legitimate use 3 — adjusting a default that doesn't fit the project

```js
Cypress.Commands.overwrite('click', (originalFn, subject, options = {}) => {
  return originalFn(subject, { scrollBehavior: 'center', ...options });
});
```

If the application has a persistent sticky header that regularly causes "covered by another element" actionability failures (Part 2.4) with the default scrollBehavior: 'top', overwriting .click() to default to 'center' project-wide is a defensible fix — though note this exact case has a simpler solution already covered in Part 1.5/3.4: the global scrollBehavior: 'center' config option achieves the same result without overwriting a command at all, and should be preferred whenever a plain config setting can do the job instead.

## The danger, made concrete

```js
// ❌ A real, subtle bug this pattern can introduce
Cypress.Commands.overwrite('should', (originalFn, subject, ...args) => {
  cy.log(`Asserting: ${args[0]}`);
  return originalFn(subject, ...args);
});
```

This looks harmless — just logging. But .should() is used internally by dozens of other built-in commands and most custom commands (including several written earlier in this very Part), and by every third-party plugin's own assertions. Adding a `cy.log()` on every single invocation can meaningfully slow down a large suite, and if the logging logic itself ever throws (a typo, an unexpected args shape from some unusual internal call), every assertion in the entire suite breaks simultaneously — not a contained failure in one test, a suite-wide outage. This is precisely the "widest blast radius" concern flagged at the top of the chapter, made concrete.

## A safer alternative to overwriting, when it's available: a new command with a new name

Before reaching for .overwrite(), ask whether a differently-named .add() achieves the same goal with none of the retroactive risk:

```js
// Instead of overwriting cy.visit() globally...
Cypress.Commands.add('visitAndLog', (url, options) => {
  cy.task('log', `Visiting: ${url}`);
  cy.visit(url, options);
});
```

This requires updating call sites to opt in explicitly, which is more work upfront but means the blast radius of any bug in the new logic is limited to exactly the tests that were deliberately updated to use it — nothing else in the suite is silently affected.

## Comparison

Playwright has no direct equivalent to `Cypress.Commands.overwrite()` at all, and the reason connects back to 8.1's comparison: because Playwright actions are plain function calls rather than named, globally-registered commands, there's no single global registry entry to intercept and rewrap. The closest analogous pattern in Playwright is wrapping the page object itself in a custom fixture that returns a proxied or extended version — considerably more code, but also considerably more explicit about exactly what's being changed and where, which somewhat mitigates the "invisible global behavior change" risk this chapter has been describing. Selenium, similarly, has no equivalent — you'd have to subclass or monkey-patch the WebElement/WebDriver class directly in your host language, which is possible but sits so far outside normal usage that it's rarely seen in real Selenium codebases.

Consolidating what's been referenced piecemeal since Part 1.3 into a complete, organized picture of how a mature project structures its support layer.

## The two files, and the one question that separates them

Both live in cypress/support/ and both auto-load before every spec (Part 1.3), but they answer different questions:

- **commands.js** answers "what new vocabulary do I want available?" — custom command definitions, as covered in 8.1 and 8.2.
- **e2e.js** answers "what needs to happen before literally every test runs, regardless of what that test is about?" — imports, global event handlers, global hooks, plugin registration.

## e2e.js, assembled from everything referenced across the manual so far

By this point in the manual, nearly every Part has added a piece to this file. Seeing it assembled in full is genuinely clarifying:

```js
// cypress/support/e2e.js

// --- Command registration (8.1) ---
import './commands';

// --- Third-party plugins ---
import '@testing-library/cypress/add-commands';   // Part 4.4
import 'cypress-real-events';                       // Part 3.4
import 'cypress-axe';                               // Part 9.7
import '@cypress/grep';                             // Part 10.1

// --- Global error handling (Part 3.14) ---
Cypress.on('uncaught:exception', (err) => {
  const benign = [
    'ResizeObserver loop limit exceeded',
    'ResizeObserver loop completed with undelivered notifications',
  ];
  if (benign.some((msg) => err.message.includes(msg))) return false;

  const thirdParty = ['googletagmanager', 'hotjar', 'segment'];
  if (thirdParty.some((s) => err.stack?.includes(s))) return false;

  return true;
});

// --- Global native-dialog defaults (Part 3.9) ---
Cypress.on('window:confirm', () => true);

// --- Global page-load hooks (Part 3.14) ---
Cypress.on('window:before:load', (win) => {
  win.localStorage.setItem('e2e-mode', 'true');
});

// --- Global suite-wide setup ---
beforeEach(() => {
  cy.intercept('GET', '**/analytics/**', { statusCode: 204 });   // silence third-party noise
});
```

Notice how much of this manual's content, read end to end, is really building toward this one file — it's the accumulated, encoded operating policy of the entire suite, loaded once and applied everywhere without any spec file needing to know it exists.

## commands.js, organized rather than accumulated

Where e2e.js tends to be short and policy-focused, commands.js tends to grow large on a real project, and organizing it deliberately matters once it passes roughly 15–20 commands. A common, effective structure is splitting by concern into separate files, all imported from commands.js:

```text
cypress/support/
├── e2e.js
├── commands.js              ← imports everything below
├── commands/
│   ├── auth.js               ← loginAs, logout, visitAuthenticated
│   ├── selectors.js          ← getByCy, getByCyLike
│   ├── api.js                ← seedEmployee, resetDatabase, interceptLeaveApi
│   ├── forms.js               ← fillForm, submitLeaveRequest
│   └── assertions.js          ← shouldHaveLeaveBalance, validateSchema

// cypress/support/commands.js
import './commands/auth';
import './commands/selectors';
import './commands/api';
import './commands/forms';
import './commands/assertions';
```

This split makes a large support layer navigable — a developer looking for the login command goes straight to commands/auth.js rather than scrolling through an undifferentiated 400-line file — and it maps naturally onto how a team might divide ownership across a growing test codebase.

## support/component.js — the parallel file for Component Testing

Briefly flagged back in Part 1.3, worth stating clearly here alongside its E2E counterpart: projects using Component Testing (Part 9.5) maintain a separate support file, because the two testing types have genuinely different setup needs — a component test doesn't need `cy.visit()`-related hooks or full-app auth setup, but does need framework-specific mounting configuration:

```js
// cypress/support/component.js
import { mount } from 'cypress/react18';
import './commands';    // shared custom commands can still be reused here

Cypress.Commands.add('mount', mount);
```

Custom commands genuinely shared between both testing types (a getByCy selector helper, for instance) can live in one shared commands.js imported by both support files; commands specific to one type (E2E's loginAs, component testing's mount wrapper) stay in their respective files.

## supportFile: false — disabling the mechanism entirely

Mentioned in passing back in Part 1.3: setting `supportFile`: false in config skips loading any support file at all.

```js
// cypress.config.js
e2e: {
  supportFile: false,
}
```

There is almost never a good reason to do this on a real project — it means no custom commands, no global error handling, no plugin registration, for every single spec. The rare legitimate case is a minimal reproduction repo built specifically to demonstrate a bug report to Cypress's maintainers or a plugin author, where the entire point is showing the absolute minimum configuration that reproduces an issue.

## beforeEach in the support file vs. beforeEach in a spec file

A distinction worth making explicit, since both are valid and the choice matters: a beforeEach inside support/e2e.js runs before every single test in every single spec file in the whole run, with no way to opt out short of restructuring the hook itself. A beforeEach inside one describe block in one spec file is scoped to that block alone (Part 2.1's hook-nesting rules). The support-file version is for something that should be genuinely universal — silencing analytics noise, as in the assembled example above — while anything specific to one feature area belongs in that feature's own spec file, not smuggled into the global support file where its purpose is far less discoverable to someone reading a failing test six months later.

## Comparison

Playwright's nearest structural equivalent to support/e2e.js is its global-setup/global-teardown files (configured in playwright.config.ts) plus base fixtures defined via test.extend() — but these are more narrowly scoped by design: global-setup runs once before the entire test run starts (not before every individual test), which is a meaningfully different lifecycle than Cypress's per-test beforeEach living in e2e.js. For genuinely per-test universal setup in Playwright, the idiomatic approach is a base fixture that every test implicitly depends on, defined once and auto-used via test.extend({ ... }) with { auto: true } — functionally comparable to Cypress's global beforeEach, but expressed through the fixture/dependency-injection system discussed in 8.1's comparison rather than a single conventional file location.
Selenium has no equivalent convention at all — global setup is whatever your test framework provides (JUnit's @BeforeAll/@BeforeEach on a shared base class every test class extends, pytest's conftest.py with autouse fixtures), and "where does project-wide policy live" is answered entirely by convention within whatever host framework you've chosen, with no Cypress-style single canonical file.

The organizational-design question that sits one level above individual custom commands — how do you structure an entire suite's worth of reusable interaction logic, and why Cypress's own official guidance actively steers away from a pattern most Selenium engineers arrive already fluent in.

## Page Object Model, briefly, for readers coming from Selenium

The Page Object Model (POM) — ubiquitous in Selenium suites — represents each page or major component as a class, encapsulating its selectors and the interactions available on it:

```js
// A Cypress POM implementation, for comparison purposes
class LeaveApprovalsPage {
  visit() { cy.visit('/leave/approvals'); return this; }
  getRows() { return cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]'); }
  approveFirst() { this.getRows().first().find('[data-cy=approve-btn]').click(); return this; }
  confirmModal() { cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click(); return this; }
}

export default new LeaveApprovalsPage();

import leaveApprovalsPage from '../support/pages/LeaveApprovalsPage';

it('approves a leave request', () => {
  leaveApprovalsPage.visit().approveFirst();
  leaveApprovalsPage.confirmModal();
  cy.get('[data-cy=toast]').should('contain', 'Approved');
});
```

This is a well-established, genuinely reasonable pattern in Selenium — and it's exactly what most engineers arriving from a Selenium background reach for instinctively when they start writing Cypress. Cypress's own official documentation explicitly recommends against it, and understanding why is more valuable than just following the recommendation blindly.

## Why POM fits Selenium's architecture and fights Cypress's

The mismatch traces directly back to Part 2.3's command queue. POM methods that return this for chaining (.visit().approveFirst()) work fine in Selenium because Selenium commands are genuinely synchronous/blocking calls against a live WebDriver session — each method call actually completes before the next line runs. In Cypress, every cy.* call inside a POM method is enqueued, not executed, per the two-phase model from Part 2.3. Chaining POM methods that internally enqueue Cypress commands doesn't cause errors, exactly — but it obscures the command queue's actual behavior behind an object-oriented facade that implies a synchronous execution model Cypress fundamentally doesn't have. This mismatch tends to surface as confusing bugs specifically around retry-ability (Part 2.4) and aliasing (Part 2.5) — a POM method that tries to return a value computed from a Cypress command runs headfirst into exactly the "can't assign command results to variables" problem from Part 2.3, now one abstraction layer removed from where a test author can see it happening.
There's a second, more practical friction: POM classes tend to accumulate selectors as private implementation detail, hidden behind method calls (getRows()), which actively works against the whole discipline built up across Part 4 — a `data-cy` selector strategy is supposed to make a test's intent legible; wrapping every selector in a class method one layer removed from the test doesn't make intent more visible, it adds a lookup step for a reader trying to understand what a failing test actually did.

## App Actions — Cypress's own recommended alternative

The pattern Cypress's documentation actually recommends is called App Actions, and it's a direct extension of everything Part 6.3 already established about blending API and UI: rather than modeling pages, you model actions a user (or the app's own state) can take, expressed as plain custom commands (8.1) that internally choose the fastest reliable mechanism — UI interaction where that's genuinely the point, direct API/state manipulation everywhere else.

```js
// cypress/support/commands/actions.js

Cypress.Commands.add('createLeaveRequest', (overrides = {}) => {
  const defaults = { employeeId: 101, type: 'sick', start: '2026-10-01', end: '2026-10-02' };
  return cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { ...defaults, ...overrides })
    .its('body');
});

Cypress.Commands.add('approveLeaveRequest', (leaveId) => {
  return cy.request('PUT', `/api/leave-requests/${leaveId}/approve`);
});

Cypress.Commands.add('approveLeaveRequestViaUi', (employeeName) => {
  cy.contains('[data-cy=leave-row]', employeeName)
    .find('[data-cy=approve-btn]')
    .click();
  cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click();
});

it('shows an approved status after approval', () => {
  cy.createLeaveRequest({ employeeId: 101 }).then((leave) => {
    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit('/leave/approvals');

    // the UI action is what's under test here — everything else was an app action
    cy.approveLeaveRequestViaUi('Aarav Sharma');

    cy.contains('[data-cy=leave-row]', 'Aarav Sharma')
      .find('[data-cy=status]').should('have.text', 'Approved');
  });
});
```

This reads as a direct, natural extension of Part 6.3's Pattern 1 and Pattern 2 — because it is one. App Actions isn't a separate philosophy bolted onto everything else in this manual; it's the organizational packaging of the exact "shortcut what isn't under test, drive through the UI what is" discipline Part 6 already built, now expressed as a deliberately named, reusable command library rather than inline `cy.request()` calls scattered through each spec.

## Why App Actions avoids POM's specific friction points

- No chained return this masking the command queue — each action command is a flat, independent custom command (8.1), returning a real Cypress chainable the way `cy.get()` or `cy.request()` naturally does, with none of the false-synchronicity implied by a fluent object-oriented interface.
- Selectors stay close to the test's actual concern. The UI-driven actions (approveLeaveRequestViaUi) still use `data-cy` selectors directly and visibly, rather than hiding them inside a class's private methods three files away from the test that depends on their exact behavior.
- It composes naturally with everything else in this manual — aliases (2.5), `cy.session()` (7.2), fixtures (5.2) — because it's just custom commands, not a competing abstraction layer with its own rules about how state and chaining work.

## A hybrid some teams land on: light structural grouping without full POM

Rejecting POM doesn't mean rejecting all organization — a common, defensible middle ground groups action commands by feature area (as shown in 8.3's commands/ folder split) without wrapping them in classes or forcing a fluent chained interface:

```js
// cypress/support/commands/leave.js
Cypress.Commands.add('createLeaveRequest', (overrides) => { /* ... */ });
Cypress.Commands.add('approveLeaveRequest', (leaveId) => { /* ... */ });
Cypress.Commands.add('rejectLeaveRequest', (leaveId, reason) => { /* ... */ });
Cypress.Commands.add('cancelLeaveRequest', (leaveId) => { /* ... */ });
```

This gets the organizational benefit POM was reaching for — related actions grouped and discoverable in one file — without introducing class instances, this-binding concerns, or the illusion of synchronous chaining that doesn't actually match how Cypress executes.

## When something POM-like is still defensible

Worth being honest rather than dogmatic: for a suite with a handful of genuinely complex, highly-stateful pages (a spreadsheet-like grid, a drag-and-drop kanban board), a thin object wrapping a set of related selectors — without the fluent return this chaining that causes the actual command-queue friction — can still be a reasonable readability aid:

```js
// A defensible, non-fluent selector grouping — not a full POM
const payrollGrid = {
  cell: (row, col) => cy.get(`[data-cy=cell-${row}-${col}]`),
  totalRow: () => cy.get('[data-cy=total-row]'),
  employeeRow: (name) => cy.contains('[data-cy=payroll-row]', name),
};

payrollGrid.employeeRow('Aarav Sharma').find('[data-cy=net-pay]').should('contain', 'NPR');
```

This avoids POM's core problem (no chained mock-synchronous methods, no command results being stored across method boundaries) while still giving a very selector-heavy page some organizational structure. The distinction that actually matters isn't "class vs. no class" — it's whether the abstraction pretends Cypress commands execute synchronously.

## Comparison

Playwright's official guidance is, somewhat notably, the opposite of Cypress's — Playwright's own documentation actively recommends Page Object Model as a good practice, and this isn't a contradiction once you trace it back to the same architectural root cause discussed in 8.1 and 8.2: Playwright's commands genuinely are await-able, so a POM method chain like await loginPage.login(user).then(() => dashboardPage.expectWelcome()) isn't hiding any false synchronicity — it's accurately representing how the underlying calls actually execute. The same organizational pattern that fights Cypress's architecture fits Playwright's naturally, which is a clean, concrete illustration of a principle worth carrying forward from this whole manual: a design pattern's fitness depends on the execution model underneath it, not on the pattern's abstract merit — POM isn't "wrong," it's wrong for Cypress specifically, for reasons that trace directly back to Part 2.3.
Selenium is POM's native, original habitat — the pattern was essentially designed for exactly Selenium's synchronous, blocking WebDriver call model, which is precisely why engineers arriving in Cypress from a Selenium background reach for it by instinct, and precisely why that instinct is worth deliberately unlearning rather than indulging.
